
  One thing worth flagging up front, since it shapes the design rather than following from it: [38;5;153mapp.js:5[39m already logs the
  username on every login attempt, and the flow it sits in handles a password. A logging subsystem wrapped around that
  flow is the easiest possible way to start shipping credentials into wherever logs land. I'll treat redaction as a
  design requirement, not a nicety.

  First question, and it's the one that decides the shape of everything else.

  [1mThe fork:[0m the two surfaces have nothing in common technically. The browser code has no filesystem, no stdout anyone
  can read in production, and needs logs [3mshipped somewhere[0m to be useful for debugging production. The Node code can
  write structured lines to stdout/stderr and let a process manager collect them — no transport, no endpoint, no privacy
  surface.

  - [1mBrowser only[0m — buys you visibility into the actual production issue if it's user-facing (the login flow). Costs a
    transport decision (where do logs go?), a batching/failure story, and the credential-redaction work. Hard to change
    later: once logs leave the browser, the destination and payload shape are a contract.
  - [1mNode only[0m — cheap and fast; structured logs to stdout, done in one small module. But if the production issues are in
    the login form, this buys you nothing.
  - [1mBoth, shared module[0m — one logger interface, two sinks. Most work, and the shared abstraction is the part most likely
    to be wrong before we know what either side actually needs.

  [1mMy recommendation: start with the browser side[0m, because "debug production issues" plus a login form points there, and
  it's the surface where there's currently no way to see anything. The Node side is easy to add afterward; the browser
  transport contract is the expensive decision, so it deserves the design attention.
